title: Implement multi-origin batches
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    This PR adds an extension pallet `pallet-utility-ext` to `pallet-utility` which allows for
    batching calls with different origins. The origins can be custom, they don't have to be
    `SystemOrigin::Signed(AccountId)`. This is done by supplying a number of batch items
    consisting of a call and its wrapping inner `TransactionExtension` to
    `pallet-utility-ext::batch_multi_origin`. The multi origin batch behaves like `batch_all`,
    meaning that all calls must succeed or the entire call will fail.

    The purpose of this PR is to enable the use case of a user (not an account) who has multiple
    transactions they would like to execute atomically in a particular order.

    Enabling this feature means configuring `pallet-utility-ext` in your runtime. Importantly, the
    pallet requires:

    - A maximum number of calls allowed in a single batch. This should generally be a low figure
      (e.g. 8 calls).
    - A call filter for which calls are allowed to be part of multi origin batches.
    - The `TransactionExtension` type which will wrap all of the calls inside the batch. This is
      different than the runtime's overarching `TransactionExtension` and is meant to be a subset
      of the items there, but without all of the extensions which are supposed to run only once
      per transaction (e.g. `CheckWeight`, `StorageReclaim`).

    A `TransactionExtension` is added to the overarching `TransactionExtension` of the runtime
    which will authorize the batch call to execute without having a "relayer" (a signer who will
    pay for the fee of the entire thing). Each item pays for itself as we have fee charging inside
    the inner tx ext pipeline. There is a small overhead because of the batch call itself which is
    split evenly among the items for fee purposes.

    The payment credit in `pallet-transaction-payment` had to change from a static single thing
    to a queue of different payment pots to accommodate for potentially many different signed
    origins re-entering during the same outer call. The pots are opened and settled in order of
    the items inside the batch.

    There are situations where individual call items are relying on the transient state in
    `pallet-transaction-payment`. This is the case today with revive calls which assume the fee
    in `pallet-transaction-payment` belongs only to the signer decoded by the ETH transaction.
    Such calls must be filtered out.
crates:
- name: pallet-utility-ext
  bump: major
- name: pallet-transaction-payment
  bump: minor
- name: polkadot-sdk
  bump: minor
